                    CEREMONIAL CLAUSE  ·  ცერემონიული პუნქტი
                          (NON-BINDING — for cultural sustenance only)

The above MIT terms are the actual license. Everything that follows has zero
legal force, but Octarinox is a Tbilisi studio with a Viking-Georgian sense of
humour, and we believe a license without character is a missed opportunity.

  By using this software you are warmly — but absolutely not legally —
  encouraged to:

    1. Pour yourself a coffee, tea, or other beverage of your choice before
       diving into a long debugging session.

    2. Say "გაუმარჯოს" (gaumarjos — "may you be victorious", a Georgian toast)
       at least once when your build finally goes green after a long red day.
       Whisper if you must. The build deserves it.

    3. If this software ever earns you actual money, consider giving a small
       portion of that money to:
         (a) someone who taught you something for free, or
         (b) an open-source project you depend on, or
         (c) the next person who asks you a beginner question.
       Pick whichever is closer.

    4. If you fork this repository and turn it into something better, please
       do not feel obligated to call it Octar Lab. We will, however, take
       it as a quiet compliment if your fork starts with the letter O.

    5. Do not use this software to build anything you would be embarrassed
       to demo to your grandmother. Surveillance tech, scams, harassment
       platforms — none of it. The MIT license technically permits it.
       The Ceremonial Clause emphatically does not.

  Violation of any clause above will result in absolutely nothing, because
  this clause is non-binding. We will be quietly disappointed, however, and
  somewhere in Tbilisi a small espresso will go cold in solidarity.

